#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:4
msgid "Documentation guidelines"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:6
msgid ""
"The ROS documentation uses reStructuredText (RST) to create consistent, "
"reviewable articles for the documentation site. This article describes the "
"main RST formatting patterns, directives, and roles used in the ROS "
"documentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:9
msgid ""
"**Area: ROS-community | Content-type: reference | Experience: beginner, "
"intermediate, expert**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:13
msgid "Table of Contents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:16
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:18
msgid ""
"When creating content for the ROS documentation, write in reStructuredText "
"(RST) and ensure that you follow good practice guidelines, or your pull "
"request may not be accepted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:21
msgid "General formatting guidelines"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:23
msgid ""
"The ROS documentation website uses the ``reStructuredText`` format, which is "
"the default plaintext markup language used by Sphinx. This section is a "
"brief introduction to ``reStructuredText`` concepts, syntax, and best "
"practices. When formatting your ``reStructuredText`` file **make sure to "
"write only one sentence per line as it makes reviewing and modifying your "
"file much easier.** Also, be mindful of the use of white space in your file! "
"The ROS documentation linter will not accept pull requests with trailing "
"white space. We recommend that you enable automatic white space highlighting "
"and cleanup if your editor supports it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:30
msgid ""
"You can refer to `reStructuredText User Documentation <https://docutils."
"sourceforge.io/rst.html>`_ for a detailed technical specification."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:32
msgid ""
"This article relates to contributing to the ROS documentation site. For more "
"information about creating or updating package documentation, see :doc:`/"
"Developer-Tools/Package-documentation/Documenting-a-ROS-2-Package`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:36
msgid "Table of contents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:38
msgid ""
"There are two types of directives used for the generation of a table of "
"contents: ``.. toctree::`` and ``.. contents::``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:40
msgid ""
"The ``.. toctree::`` directive is used in top-level pages like ``Tutorials."
"rst`` to set ordering and visibility of its child pages. This directive "
"creates both left navigation panel and in-page navigation links to the child "
"pages listed. It helps readers to understand the structure of separate "
"documentation sections and navigate between pages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:44
msgid ""
".. toctree::\n"
"   :maxdepth: 1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:49
msgid ""
"The ``.. contents::`` directive is used for the generation of a table of "
"contents for that particular page. It parses all present headings in a page "
"and builds an in-page nested table of contents. It helps readers to see an "
"overview of the content and navigate inside a page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:53
msgid ""
"The ``.. contents::`` directive supports the definition of maximum depth of "
"nested sections. Using ``:depth: 2`` will only show sections and subsections "
"in the table of contents."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:56
msgid ""
".. contents:: Table of Contents\n"
"   :depth: 2\n"
"   :local:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:63
msgid "Headings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:65
msgid ""
"There are four main heading types used in the documentation. Note that the "
"number of symbols has to match the length of the title."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:68
msgid ""
"Page Title Header\n"
"=================\n"
"\n"
"Section Header\n"
"--------------\n"
"\n"
"2 Subsection Header\n"
"^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n"
"\n"
"2.4 Subsubsection Header\n"
"~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:82
msgid ""
"We usually use one digit for numbering subsections and two digits (dot "
"separated) for numbering subsubsections in tutorials and how-to guides."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:85
msgid "Lists"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:87
msgid ""
"Stars ``*`` are used for listing unordered items with bullet points, and "
"number sign ``#.``  is used for listing numbered items. Both of them support "
"nested definitions and will render accordingly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:90
msgid ""
"* bullet point\n"
"\n"
"  * bullet point nested\n"
"  * bullet point nested\n"
"\n"
"* bullet point"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:99
msgid ""
"#. first listed item\n"
"#. second lited item"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:105
msgid "Code formatting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:107
#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:109
msgid ""
"In-text code can be formatted using ``backticks`` for showing "
"``highlighted`` code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:113
msgid ""
"Code blocks inside a page need to be captured using ``.. code-block::`` "
"`directives <https://www.sphinx-doc.org/en/master/usage/restructuredtext/"
"directives.html#directive-code-block>`_. ``.. code-block::`` supports code "
"highlighting for syntaxes like ``C++``, ``YAML``, ``console``, ``bash``, and "
"more. Code inside the directive needs to be indented."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:117
msgid ""
".. code-block:: C++\n"
"\n"
"   int main(int argc, char** argv)\n"
"   {\n"
"      rclcpp::init(argc, argv);\n"
"      rclcpp::spin(std::make_shared<ParametersClass>());\n"
"      rclcpp::shutdown();\n"
"      return 0;\n"
"   }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:130
msgid "Code blocks: ``bash`` vs. ``console``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:132
msgid ""
"``bash`` and ``console`` are similar, but they serve two different purposes. "
"Choosing the right one is important to ensure that the content is formatted "
"correctly and that the copy button copies the right content. Below is an "
"explanation of each one; skip to the end of this section for a list of use-"
"cases and corresponding examples."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:136
msgid ""
"``bash`` is meant for scripts, e.g., for bash commands from a script file. "
"Example result:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:139
#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:174
msgid ""
"export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=42\n"
"ros2 run turtlesim turtlesim_node"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:144
msgid ""
"``console`` is meant for commands to be run in a terminal, optionally "
"including their output. This makes it clear that the given commands need to "
"be run in a terminal. It also allows separating command lines from output "
"lines using prompt symbols such as ``$`` or ``#``. Command lines are "
"formatted as bash commands while output lines are formatted as normal text. "
"The prompt symbol is not selectable, and clicking on the copy button in the "
"upper right-hand corner copies *only* the commands, not the outputs nor the "
"prompt symbols. This means that if a ``console`` code block is used without "
"any ``$``, the copy button will not copy any lines. Example result:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:152
#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:161
msgid ""
"$ export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=42\n"
"$ ros2 run turtlesim turtlesim_node --ros-args --remap \"__node:"
"=my_turtle\"\n"
"[INFO] [1742150439.022947971] [my_turtle]: Starting turtlesim with node "
"name /my_turtle\n"
"[INFO] [1742150439.026043867] [my_turtle]: Spawning turtle [turtle1] at "
"x=[5.544445], y=[5.544445], theta=[0.000000]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:159
msgid "Compare the above with a ``bash`` code-block:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:168
msgid ""
"To simplify code blocks, ``bash`` can still be used without ``$`` for "
"commands meant to be run in a terminal if the code block does not include "
"any output lines. To help choose between ``bash`` and ``console``, see the "
"following list of use-cases and corresponding examples:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:171
msgid ""
"Commands meant to be copied into a script file. Use ``.. code-block:: bash`` "
"without ``$``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:179
msgid ""
"Commands meant to be run in a terminal. It is highly recommended to use ``.. "
"code-block:: console`` with ``$`` on all command lines for consistency and "
"clarity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:182
msgid ""
"If there is output that needs to be displayed, include it in the same block:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:184
msgid ""
"$ source /opt/ros/rolling/setup.bash\n"
"$ ros2 run turtlesim turtlesim_node\n"
"[INFO] [1743878028.269334696] [turtlesim]: Starting turtlesim with node "
"name /turtlesim\n"
"[INFO] [1743878028.275096618] [turtlesim]: Spawning turtle [turtle1] at "
"x=[5.544445], y=[5.544445], theta=[0.000000]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:193
msgid ""
"If some output lines start with ``#``, it is crucial to separate commands "
"from their output because the ``#`` symbol is used to denote a command. "
"Therefore, place the output in a separate ``.. code-block:: text``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:197
msgid "Images"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:199
msgid "Images can be inserted using the ``.. image::`` directive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:201
msgid ".. image:: images/turtlesim_follow1.png"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:205
msgid ""
"In this case, the image file (``turtlesim_follow1.png``) is located in the "
"``images/`` directory relative to the ``.rst`` file that uses the image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:207
msgid ""
"However, all image files end up in an ``_images/`` directory relative to the "
"root of the docs. Therefore, when using ``:target:`` to add a hyperlink to "
"the image file, use a relative link going up to the root directory and then "
"down to the ``_images/`` directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:210
msgid ""
".. image:: images/turtlesim_follow1.png\n"
"   :target: ../../_images/turtlesim_follow1.png"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:216
msgid "Charts, graphs, and diagrams"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:218
msgid ""
"ROS documentation now supports charts, graphs, and diagrams written using "
"`Mermaid Charts. <https://mermaid.js.org/intro/>`__ We prefer that charts, "
"graphs, and diagrams use Mermaid instead of static image files as it allows "
"us to programmatically update and edit these resources as the project "
"evolves. Full documentation of the Mermaid graph language syntax can be "
"found `on their website. <https://mermaid.js.org/intro/syntax-reference."
"html>`__"
msgstr ""

